{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2688 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "64fec85a891607408932c90e5237345dec11caec6210317bb358132b005a31eb" :edb/db-before-t 2687 :edb/db-after-t 2688 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536000 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:35:58.099Z" 13194139536000 true] [17592186198361 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378944 13194139536000 true] [17592186198362 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378930 13194139536000 true] [17592186198362 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378937 13194139536000 true] [17592186198572 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536000 true] [17592186198572 1100 "For the function of two variables\n\n$f(x, y) = e^{x - 2y}$,\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0, 0)$ is given by\n$f(x, y) = 1 + x + ay + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + bxy + cy^{2}$.\nFind the value of $a + b + c$." 13194139536000 false] [17592186198572 1100 "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{x-2y},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=1+x+a\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139536000 true] [17592186198572 1145 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =4{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+1\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+4\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+x+(-2)y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-2)+(-2)+2=-2.\n$$" 13194139536000 true] [17592186198605 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536000 true] [17592186198605 1100 "For the twice-differentiable function $f: R^{2} → R$, we have the following data:\n$f(1, 0)|= 3, \\mid f^{′}(1, 0)|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\mid f^{″}(1, 0)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1, 2)$." 13194139536000 false] [17592186198605 1100 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =3, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,2).$" 13194139536000 true] [17592186198605 1145 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =3, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & ≈3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(4) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378930 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378931 13194139536000 true] [17592186378930 1089 "q-163102" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378930 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536000 true] [17592186378930 1100 "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." 13194139536000 true] [17592186378930 1113 #uuid "9ab3d7fc-b7a1-5808-8e28-249275bd1920" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378930 1145 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y & \\frac{x}{y}+x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}+1 & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n2+(-1)+(x-1)=x.\n$$" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378932 13194139536000 true] [17592186378931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378933 13194139536000 true] [17592186378931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378934 13194139536000 true] [17592186378931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378935 13194139536000 true] [17592186378931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378936 13194139536000 true] [17592186378931 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536000 true] [17592186378931 1041 "selection" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378931 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378935 13194139536000 true] [17592186378931 1110 #uuid "b44c59d0-6099-5fc7-9331-ce2d8d5038d2" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378932 1034 "x+1" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378932 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536000 true] [17592186378932 1111 #uuid "5b4da1ac-a9a9-5994-bf6d-f8dff33e935b" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378933 1034 "x+3" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378933 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536000 true] [17592186378933 1111 #uuid "f3296e2c-c848-58b7-95ad-1c12a1a0c525" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378934 1034 "x-1" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378934 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536000 true] [17592186378934 1111 #uuid "77d07acf-85bf-569d-8a97-b30f8e63c624" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378935 1034 "x" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378935 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536000 true] [17592186378935 1111 #uuid "83af7234-dfd4-5042-a19c-3de99d6cada7" 13194139536000 true] [17592186378936 